    "source_record.json": {
      "background": "Collected from Oberwolfach Reports, which document mathematical workshops and their open-problem discussions.\n\n**Source report:** [Real Algebraic Geometry with a View Toward Hyperbolic Programming and Free Probability (2021), Oberwolfach Reports, DOI 10.4171/owr/2020/12, pp. 639--712](https://doi.org/10.4171/owr/2020/12)\n\n## Literature review (checked 2026-08-22)\n\n**Status:** partially solved\n\n**Assessment.** The existence part is solved affirmatively by the Chebyshev morphism, which has only real periodic points. The question whether the corresponding locus has nonempty interior or full dimension remains open; only lower-dimensional families are known.\n\n**Sources checked.**\n\n- [Kozhasov--Kummer (2020)](https://arxiv.org/abs/2004.10003)\n- [Oberwolfach Report 12/2020](https://doi.org/10.4171/owr/2020/12)\n\nThis is a dated literature triage derived from the supplied OWR curation; verify time-sensitive status and cited claims independently.",
      "category": {
        "created_at": "2026-07-31T15:26:25.671Z",
        "description": "Problems about long-term behavior of deterministic systems, Hamiltonian dynamics, and periodic orbits.",
        "display_name": "Dynamical Systems",
        "id": 11,
        "name": "dynamical_systems",
        "order_index": 11,
        "slug": "dynamical-systems"
      },
      "category_id": 11,
      "clean_statement": "For $d\\geq2$, let $\\mathcal R_d$ be the set of real morphisms $\\phi:\\mathbb P^1\\to\\mathbb P^1$ of degree $d$ whose periodic points are all real. The existence question has an affirmative answer via Chebyshev maps; does $\\mathcal R_d$ have full dimension, equivalently nonempty interior, in the space of real degree-$d$ morphisms?",
      "created_at": "2026-08-25T00:00:00Z",
      "difficulty": {
        "color_class": "text-yellow-600 bg-yellow-50 border-yellow-200",
        "description": "Difficult problems requiring specialized knowledge and sophisticated techniques.",
        "id": 3,
        "level": 3,
        "name": "L3: Advanced"
      },
      "difficulty_level_id": 3,
      "favorite_count": 0,
      "id": 30004438,
      "literature_assessment": "The existence part is solved affirmatively by the Chebyshev morphism, which has only real periodic points. The question whether the corresponding locus has nonempty interior or full dimension remains open; only lower-dimensional families are known.",
      "literature_checked_at": "2026-08-22",
      "literature_sources": [
        {
          "label": "Kozhasov--Kummer (2020)",
          "url": "https://arxiv.org/abs/2004.10003"
        },
        {
          "label": "Oberwolfach Report 12/2020",
          "url": "https://doi.org/10.4171/owr/2020/12"
        }
      ],
      "original_statement": "For d\\ge2, does there exist a real morphism \\phi:\\mathbb{P}^1\\to\\mathbb{P}^1 of degree d with only real periodic points, and does the set of such real morphisms have full dimension, equivalently nonempty interior, in the space of all real degree-d morphisms?",
      "problem_number": "OWR-17475-003",
      "proposed_by": null,
      "proposed_year": 2020,
      "published": true,
      "set": {
